// Roughly what the GC does on every allocation:
// 1. New objects go into Gen0.
// 2. Gen0 fills up -> a Gen0 collection runs (cheap, scans only Gen0).
// 3. Anything still referenced survives and is promoted to Gen1, then Gen2.
// 4. Gen2 (and the Large Object Heap) is collected far less often, at higher cost.

GC.Collect(0); // force a Gen0-only collection (rarely needed in real code)

